WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.582
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level two.

2
00:00:05.882 --> 00:00:09.536
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.836 --> 00:00:16.238
Unit five: Labor Market. Today you will describe ongoing change with the present perfect continuous.

4
00:00:16.538 --> 00:00:19.318
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:23.616
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:25.997
Unemployment rate.

7
00:00:26.983 --> 00:00:31.467
Unemployment rate means the percentage of workers who have no job but want one.

8
00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:34.501
The unemployment rate fell to four percent.

9
00:00:35.117 --> 00:00:37.885
Your turn. Say: Unemployment rate.

10
00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:42.199
Unemployment rate.

11
00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:44.021
Job market.

12
00:00:45.017 --> 00:00:48.166
Job market means the supply of and demand for workers.

13
00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.214
The job market is strong for engineers.

14
00:00:51.817 --> 00:00:54.864
Your turn. Say: Job market.

15
00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:58.806
Job market.

16
00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:00.865
Minimum wage.

17
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:05.742
Minimum wage means the lowest pay an employer may legally give.

18
00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:08.280
The minimum wage rose in January.

19
00:01:08.883 --> 00:01:11.710
Your turn. Say: Minimum wage.

20
00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:15.951
Minimum wage.

21
00:01:16.717 --> 00:01:17.973
Workforce.

22
00:01:18.983 --> 00:01:22.573
Workforce means all the people who work in a company or country.

23
00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:25.067
A young workforce drives growth.

24
00:01:25.683 --> 00:01:28.173
Your turn. Say: Workforce.

25
00:01:31.033 --> 00:01:32.104
Workforce.

26
00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:33.874
Labor union.

27
00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:38.740
Labor union means an organisation that defends workers' rights.

28
00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:42.185
The labor union negotiated better conditions.

29
00:01:42.783 --> 00:01:45.447
Your turn. Say: Labor union.

30
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:49.522
Labor union.

31
00:01:50.283 --> 00:01:51.540
Skill gap.

32
00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:56.941
Skill gap means the difference between skills employers need and workers have.

33
00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:00.026
A skill gap slows many factories.

34
00:02:00.617 --> 00:02:03.559
Your turn. Say: Skill gap.

35
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:07.796
Skill gap.

36
00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.120
Employment contract.

37
00:02:11.117 --> 00:02:15.160
Employment contract means a written agreement between employer and employee.

38
00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.295
Read the employment contract carefully.

39
00:02:18.883 --> 00:02:21.896
Your turn. Say: Employment contract.

40
00:02:24.733 --> 00:02:26.408
Employment contract.

41
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:28.244
Remote work.

42
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:33.560
Remote work means working from home or another place away from the office.

43
00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:36.277
Remote work has changed city life.

44
00:02:36.883 --> 00:02:39.663
Your turn. Say: Remote work.

45
00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:43.640
Remote work.

46
00:02:44.433 --> 00:02:48.212
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:54.709
Visal, the paper says 'Companies have been hiring remotely since the pandemic.' Why 'have been hiring'?

48
00:02:55.817 --> 00:03:06.619
The present perfect continuous shows an action that began in the past and is still in progress. It stresses the process, not just the result.

49
00:03:07.717 --> 00:03:09.554
What other labor news uses it?

50
00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:18.736
'Unions have been demanding a higher minimum wage' or 'The skill gap has been widening for years.'

51
00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:21.861
Does it change the job market?

52
00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:31.570
Yes. Remote work has been reshaping the workforce, and employment contracts have been changing to match.

53
00:03:32.733 --> 00:03:34.187
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:38.965
Grammar focus: Present perfect continuous for ongoing change.

55
00:03:39.265 --> 00:03:40.464
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:44.125
Companies have been hiring remotely since the pandemic.

57
00:03:45.017 --> 00:03:47.457
The skill gap has been widening for years.

58
00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:51.244
Unions have been demanding a higher minimum wage.

59
00:03:52.200 --> 00:04:01.191
Use have/has been + -ing for actions that started in the past and continue now, often with for or since. State verbs (know, own) do not take this form.

60
00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:05.624
Be careful! Don't say: Unions have been demand more pay.

61
00:04:07.567 --> 00:04:10.100
Say: Unions have been demanding more pay.

62
00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:11.924
Use -ing after have been.

63
00:04:12.733 --> 00:04:15.107
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:17.482
A Changing Workplace.

65
00:04:17.782 --> 00:04:21.047
The labor market has been changing quickly over the past decade.

66
00:04:22.033 --> 00:04:27.655
Remote work has been reshaping the workforce, and many employment contracts now allow flexible hours.

67
00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:35.450
A labor union has been campaigning for a higher minimum wage, while employers have been complaining about a skill gap.

68
00:04:36.433 --> 00:04:40.941
The unemployment rate has been falling slowly, but good jobs are not equally available.

69
00:04:41.933 --> 00:04:44.537
Workers and employers will need to keep adapting.

70
00:04:45.633 --> 00:04:49.693
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:52.968
The skill gap blank widening for years.

72
00:04:53.268 --> 00:04:56.967
A, has been. B, is been. C, have being.

73
00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:02.440
The answer is A: has been.

74
00:05:03.583 --> 00:05:07.092
The lowest pay an employer may legally give is the blank.

75
00:05:07.392 --> 00:05:11.406
A, minimum wage. B, workforce. C, contract.

76
00:05:14.967 --> 00:05:17.164
The answer is A: minimum wage.

77
00:05:18.317 --> 00:05:21.663
An organisation that defends workers' rights is a labor blank.

78
00:05:21.963 --> 00:05:25.140
A, union. B, gap. C, market.

79
00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:30.525
The answer is A: union.

80
00:05:31.683 --> 00:05:33.486
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:33.786 --> 00:05:40.957
A, Unions have been demand higher pay.. B, Unions have been demanding higher pay.. C, Unions has been demanding higher pay..

82
00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:47.939
The answer is B: Unions have been demanding higher pay..

83
00:05:49.083 --> 00:05:52.372
According to the reading, the unemployment rate has been…

84
00:05:52.672 --> 00:05:57.323
A, rising fast. B, falling slowly. C, constant.

85
00:06:00.867 --> 00:06:03.540
The answer is B: falling slowly.

86
00:06:04.733 --> 00:06:07.862
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:08.717 --> 00:06:11.448
How has remote work changed the modern labor market?

88
00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:21.901
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:22.201 --> 00:06:46.802
Remote work has opened the labor market to people who live far from big cities, so employers can hire from a wider pool. It has also saved commuting time, although some workers feel isolated. Companies have been rewriting employment contracts to include flexible hours. However, jobs that need physical presence cannot benefit in the same way.

90
00:06:47.102 --> 00:06:48.927
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:49.817 --> 00:06:55.708
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and present perfect continuous for ongoing change.

92
00:06:56.008 --> 00:07:01.041
Today's value is adaptability. Be ready for rain or sun and change your plans.

93
00:07:01.341 --> 00:07:03.888
See you in Unit six. Goodbye!

94
00:07:04.188 --> 00:07:06.992
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

